+All evidence below is from this run's events, stage transcripts, and journal (run 01M1YS4NJH27NS7CQ02AWM9BNE, seed fabro-c381, all stages green first-pass, $0.133 total, 2m53s wall). Ordered by expected impact.
+
+## 1. The in-flight-PR guard is structurally disabled on this host — fix the tool, not the prompt
+**What happened:** planner step 4 mandates `gh pr list --state open`; the call failed with exit 127 `gh: command not found` (event seq 40–41), degraded fail-open per policy, and the journal recorded "future runs on this host cannot audit PR double-picks." The run's engine side clearly has GitHub access (PR #51 was created), only the `fabro-toolchain:noble` sandbox lacks `gh`.
+**Change:** bake `gh` into `.fabro/Dockerfile` (toolchain image), or move the open-PR check engine-side into the run's own PR metadata instead of a sandbox binary.
+**Expected effect:** the double-pick guard (which exists because fabro-22e4 re-implemented a fix already waiting in PR #47 — a whole wasted run) actually functions; also removes one guaranteed erroring tool call per planning pass.
+
+## 2. Planner eats a 160-seed firehose to pick one seed — bound the listing
+**What happened:** `sd ready --assignee fabro --limit 200` returned 21,270 bytes / 160 issues into the planner's context (event seq 33); the model then picked the top entry. Planner was **$0.065 of $0.133 total (49% of LLM cost) and 46s of 112s active time** — the most expensive stage for a near-deterministic decision.
+**Change:** in `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md` step 1, change the command to `sd ready --assignee fabro --limit 200 | head -15` (keeps the fabro-c16d no-truncation fix at the CLI level, bounds context); the deeper fix is filed seed fabro-c3b4 / fabro-0da8 — a deterministic pre-planner script node (same pattern as `gatebounce`) that emits top-3 candidates + extracted `Basis:` paths as context.
+**Expected effect:** cuts planner input tokens and round-trips substantially; planner is the single biggest cost lever in the graph.
+
+## 3. Zero cache writes across all three agent stages — share the prompt prefix
+**What happened:** billing shows `cache_write_tokens: 0` for planner, implementer, and reviewer, while `cache_read_tokens` hit 183k — each stage re-pays for the repeated prefix (goal text, ~13k-char role prompt, 24KB AGENTS.md memory).
+**Change:** engine-side shared prompt-cache prefix across stages in one run (already filed as fabro-944d).
+**Expected effect:** direct input-cost reduction on every multi-agent run; the repeated AGENTS.md + preamble is identical byte-for-byte across stages here.
+
+## 4. Reviewer paid 42% of its input for memory it didn't use
+**What happened:** reviewer context breakdown: `memory` = 5,684 of 13,553 input tokens (AGENTS.md, 23,967 bytes, loaded into all three agent stages per `agent.memory.loaded` events). The reviewer's actual job this pass was judging a 15-line prompt diff against a bulleted brief — it needed neither repo-wide conventions nor Rust build rules.
+**Change:** per-node memory scoping (engine; sibling of filed fabro-9588 for skills) — reviewer gets a slim subset or none; planner keeps the full file.
+**Expected effect:** ~5.7k tokens saved on the reviewer (and re-check scoping for the implementer on docs-only seeds); meaningful slice of the 47k billed input tokens.
+
+## 5. Mandated redundant `sd show` re-fetch in the implementer
+**What happened:** the planner's brief already carried the complete distilled spec, yet implementer step 1 ("Re-read the seed requirements from `sd show`") forced another `sd show fabro-c381 --format json | head -80` call (event seq 97) that added nothing.
+**Change:** in `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/implementer.md` step 1, make the re-fetch conditional: "only when the brief is thin or marked verification-only" (this is filed fabro-a67f/fabro-4881; it just hasn't landed).
+**Expected effect:** one fewer tool call + LLM round-trip per implementer pass; smaller risk of the raw seed body contradicting the (authoritative, contradiction-resolved) brief.
+
+## 6. ~10% of wall time is stage-boundary overhead — skip metadata snapshots on non-agent stages
+**What happened:** metadata snapshot took 2.0s at init and 2.3s after the planner checkpoint; observable gaps of ~5–6s sit between every stage pair (implementer→tester→evidence→reviewer→closeout) from checkpoint commits + pushes + snapshots. On a 173s run that's roughly 15–20s of pure bookkeeping.
+**Change:** make checkpoint metadata snapshots async or agent-stage-only (filed fabro-c2ca/fabro-cf03) — the tester/evidence/closeout snapshots here carried no agent state worth 2s each.
+**Expected effect:** ~10% wall-time cut on short single-seed runs; proportionally more as gates get faster.
+
+## 7. fs_hide guidance is buried where the model doesn't look before grepping
+**What happened:** despite planner.md step 3 saying "never burn tool calls discovering the denial," the planner's first basis check was a `grep` on `.fabro` → `path is hidden by fs_hide` (seq 47–48), one wasted call + LLM round-trip before switching to shell `rg`. The instruction lives mid-step in a ~13k-char prompt; grep is the instinctive first move.
+**Change:** in `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md`, hoist a one-line tool rule to the top of the "sd command reference" section: "`.fabro/**` is fs_hide for you — grep/glob/read_file on it always fail; use shell (`rg`, `sed`) for basis checks." (Engine alternative, filed fabro-8296, returns the notice from glob too.)
+**Expected effect:** one fewer wasted round-trip (~3–5s) per planning pass; more importantly, removes the failure mode where the model misreads the denial as "path doesn't exist" and wrongly closes a seed as stale-basis.
